# No em dashes in user-facing text

Never use em dashes in student-facing or UI copy. This includes:

- The em dash character `—` (U+2014)
- The en dash `–` (U+2013) used as punctuation or in force names
- ASCII stand-ins that act like an em dash: ` - ` (space-hyphen-space) as a clause break

## Prefer instead

- A period and a new sentence
- A comma
- A colon
- Parentheses
- A middot `·` for chrome separators (already used in this suite)

## Examples

```text
❌ Intermolecular forces are attractions between molecules - weaker than bonds.
✅ Intermolecular forces are attractions between molecules. They are weaker than bonds.

❌ Orientation matters - turn a molecule and the pull can flip.
✅ Orientation matters. Turn a molecule and the pull can flip.

❌ Dipole–dipole / Ion–induced dipole
✅ Dipole-dipole / Ion-induced dipole
```

## Allowed hyphens

Regular ASCII hyphens `-` are fine inside compound words and force names (`dipole-dipole`, `ion-dipole`, `H-bond`) and in code/math.

## Scope

Applies to all user-facing strings: HTML copy, canvas/HUD labels, buttons, `pairs.js` force labels, status text, titles, TOC. Internal code comments may keep plain hyphens; still avoid `—`.
